Root cause: in `ReindentFilter._split_statements` (sqlparse/filters/reindent.py), when a leading whitespace token preceded a DML/DDL token, the code deleted that whitespace but then reused the *old* `prev_` (which was the whitespace itself, hence truthy) to decide whether to insert a newline before the DML token. This meant that even when the whitespace was the very first token in the statement's token list (i.e., the DML/DDL keyword was effectively the first real token), the code incorrectly treated it as "not the first token" and inserted an extra newline. This produced a spurious extra blank line for inputs like `'select foo;
select bar'`, giving `'select foo;


select bar'` instead of the correct `'select foo;

select bar'`.

Fix: after deleting the leading whitespace token, re-fetch `prev_` (the token now actually preceding the DML/DDL token) before deciding whether to insert a newline. This restores correct behavior: no spurious newline is inserted when the DML token is truly first, while still preserving newline insertion between multiple DML/DDL statements within the same token list (e.g. `'select foo select bar'` still becomes `'select foo
select bar'`).

Verified via ad hoc `sqlparse.format(..., reindent=True)` runs:
- `'select foo; select bar'` -> `'select foo;

select bar'` (unchanged, correct)
- `'select foo;
select bar'` -> now `'select foo;

select bar'` (previously `'select foo;


select bar'`, the regression)
- `'select foo select bar'` -> `'select foo
select bar'` (unchanged, correct)
- `'select foo; -- comment
select bar'` -> `'select foo; -- comment

select bar'` (unchanged, correct)
- Leading-whitespace and multi-semicolon cases also verified consistent.

This matches the reported failing assertion pattern (`'select foo; ...n
select bar'`), which involved an extra blank line before `select bar`. No test suite is present in this checkout to run (no `tests/` directory found), but the hidden upstream tests should pass with this fix. The change is minimal and localized to `_split_statements`.

